The baseline does one ct-pt multiply (with rescale) and one plaintext add (which needs encoding at the product's scale). There is little left to cut, so the only question is whether the plaintext-add encoding or the rescale can be avoided.

An alternative is to fold B into the multiplication, but that requires x to carry a constant slot, which isn't available. Another option is to leave the add as a plaintext add before the rescale. CHEHAB probably already does this, so the circuit would not change.

The one structural change I can try is to compute (x + B/A) * A. That needs a plaintext division, which is plaintext-plaintext arithmetic and is not allowed. So I'll try a variant that adds B after the multiply as a ciphertext-plaintext add and see whether CHEHAB handles it differently, for example by writing it as `a * x` and `b + ...`. This is likely normalized to the same circuit, but it is the only equivalent form available, so I'm submitting it.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = a * x;
  Ciphertext y = b + t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline is probably already at the minimum for this function: one plaintext multiply with its rescale, plus one add. Every other equivalent form I can write needs plaintext-plaintext arithmetic (such as B/A or A·B), which CKKS rules forbid, or it adds operations.

I'm resubmitting the baseline circuit, in the lowest-cost form I can find. I expect it to be flagged as identical to the baseline again, and I don't see a legal rewrite that is cheaper.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't have a legal rewrite that changes the circuit and lowers its cost. Any other form either needs plaintext-plaintext arithmetic or adds operations on top of the single plaintext multiply, rescale and add. The best valid kernel is still the baseline form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This attempt uses a different circuit: negate x, multiply by A, subtract B, then negate again. It adds two cheap ciphertext negations but keeps a single plaintext multiply, rescale and plaintext add or subtract. It is unlikely to be faster, but it is a legal structural variant.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext nx = -x;
  Ciphertext t = nx * a - b;
  Ciphertext y = -t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This variant uses one negation instead of two: compute (−x)·A and then B − (−x·A). It is still a legal rewrite, with a single plaintext multiply and one plaintext add or subtract.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = (-x) * a;
  Ciphertext y = b - t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't see a cheaper circuit. The baseline already has the minimum: one plaintext multiply with its rescale, and one add. Every alternative I can write legally adds operations, as attempt 4 did, or needs forbidden plaintext-plaintext arithmetic. I'm submitting the baseline form again.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no further structural idea that reduces the circuit. The baseline's single plaintext multiply with rescale, plus one add, is the minimum for y = x·A + B under the CKKS rules. I'm keeping that form as my final answer.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I found no cheaper circuit for this task. Seven attempts either reproduced the baseline circuit or, in attempt 4, added operations and measured 10.07 ms against the baseline's 9.68 ms. For y = x·A + B, the baseline's one plaintext multiply (with its rescale) and one add is the minimum. Any cheaper form would need plaintext-plaintext arithmetic, such as B/A or A·B, and CKKS forbids that.

I'm submitting the baseline form for the last attempt.

